# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. REGRESSION GUARD: p-values near the centre of a large-n distribution.
#
# This is where the first of the two bugs lived.  Every tail here goes through
# the regularized incomplete beta, whose continued fraction needs roughly
# 1.3*sqrt(a+b) terms at a+b ~ 1e3 falling to 0.25*sqrt(a+b) by a+b ~ 1e7 -- so
# the flat 500-iteration cap it used to carry silently truncated once n got
# large.  At n = 2.1e7 that left a relative error of 9e-5: binom_test(10079990,
# 21000000, p => 0.48) returned 0.996781946606 where R and SciPy both say
# 0.9966892187965, i.e. it was wrong in the fourth decimal of a printed
# p-value.  The cap now scales with sqrt(a+b).
#
# The same cases also exercised the second half of that fix.  The front factor
# x^a (1-x)^b / B(a,b) used to come from exp() of differenced lgamma()s; at
# a = b = 1e7 those are each ~1.6e8 and cancel down to ~1.5e7, so their last
# bits are worth 4e-8 in the exponent and therefore 4e-8 relatively in the
# result.  It is now built from Loader's saddle-point form (stirlerr/bd0), which
# never forms the large intermediates -- the same construction binom_test's own
# dbinom already used.  Combined, these cases went from 9e-5 to 3.3e-13.
#
# x is placed at the mean and just off it, so both the x < m and x > m branches
# of the two-sided rule run with a large support behind them.
#
# [ x, n, p, alternative, p-value ]
